;; The reference set `bb perf` gates against — the performance counterpart of
;; the committed visual baselines under tools/visual-tests/**-snapshots/.
;; Committed, reviewed in the diff, refreshed only by an explicit
;; `bb perf-update` that a human then commits.
;;
;; GENERATED by `bb perf-update` — edit :why freely (it is preserved), but this
;; header is re-emitted from scripts/perf.clj on every write.
;;
;; Every entry is a COUNT of a structural event, never a duration. That is the
;; whole design, not a simplification: `1` is `1` on a laptop, on ubuntu-latest,
;; and at the load average 75 that scripts/ci.clj once measured while timing out
;; 9 of 17 checks. docs/PERF_NOTES.md records a genuine optimisation measuring
;; "~5% — inside the run's std-dev"; counts have no std-dev. Wall-clock numbers
;; live in perf/runs/*.edn and are reported, never gated.
;;
;; ONLY invariants belong here — counters that do not move when somebody adds a
;; test. `:registry/delta-recompile` (346 across the unit suite) and
;; `:registry/rebuild` (107) grow with the namespace count; gating them would
;; fail honest PRs and train everyone to re-baseline without reading, which is
;; how a gate becomes a rubber stamp. They are reported as trend data instead.
;;
;; :max is a CEILING. Exceeding it fails `bb perf`, and the :why is what gets
;; printed — write it for whoever sees the failure with no context.

{:unit
 {:fixture/container-boot
  {:max 1,
   :why
   "A second PostgreSQL container means some caller bypassed get-container's double-checked lock. Costs ~3 s and breaks the per-NS logical-DB isolation the whole shared-container design rests on."},
  :fixture/golden-bootstrap
  {:max 1,
   :why
   "The golden bootstrap is ~14 s and is cached per JVM x package-set. A second one means a namespace asked for a package set nobody else uses — check its `packages` vector before accepting the cost."},
  :fixture/type-check-sweep
  {:max 0,
   :why
   "The topological type-check sweep is ~40 s, the single most expensive fixture step there is. The unit suite has never needed one. If this fires, a namespace started bootstrapping with :skip-type-check? false — that is what 6840f542 removed."},
  :registry/delta-fell-back-to-rebuild
  {:max 0,
   :why
   "A caller named its changed fn-ids and STILL paid for a full graph recompile: delta-recompile! hit a cold reverse-deps index and silently called rebuild!. Nothing in the return value says so and no timing distinguishes it from a cold cache — this counter is the only evidence. It is the exact failure 9d66adc0 and 0b74f1dc were about; measured at 4137 fns, the rebuild it hides costs 49.8 s."}},
 :perf
 #:sql{:graph-entities-tree
       {:max 1,
        :why
        "The sidebar's first paint. 1 round trip, steady-state (the compile is paid by whichever scenario runs first — see bb.edn's test-perf note on --no-randomize). This endpoint was 95 ms / 4.5 MB before it was scoped down to ?scope=tree; if this climbs, the scoping has sprung a leak and the sidebar is reading rows it does not paint."},
       :create-fn
       {:max 19,
        :why
        "Creating one :fn costs 20 round trips, steady-state — it runs first, so it pays the compile reads for the suite. A jump means the write path started re-reading what it already had: this operation once spent 477 ms of its 918 ms re-reading the whole graph to recompile a closure whose blast radius was one fn (9c1b882a spliced the cache instead)."}},
 :trend
 #:trend{:graph-entities-tree
         {:units 19.59,
          :band 2.0,
          :why
          "The sidebar's first paint got materially slower relative to a round trip. It issues only ONE query, so anything here is compile/serialisation work, not the database."},
         :create-fn
         {:units 2043.46,
          :band 2.0,
          :why
          "Creating one :fn costs thousands of round trips' worth of time against only 20 actual queries — the time is in the compile, not the SQL, exactly as PERF_NOTES describes (477 ms of 918 ms re-reading the graph). A jump here means the compile path regressed."}}}
